/**
@defgroup api API Reference
@brief Everything a program linking against libmp3lame may use.

The public interface is declared in @c include/lame.h, the only header LAME
installs. Nothing else in the source tree is part of the interface, and
nothing else carries a stability or ABI guarantee.

The sections below are in the order a program uses them: create an encoder,
choose the encoding parameters, then feed audio and collect MP3 frames.
Tagging is set up before the first frame is encoded, and the statistics are
read at the end, before the encoder is released. The decoder is independent of
all of it.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_encoding Encoding
@ingroup api
@brief Creating an encoder, running it, and releasing it.

The encoder lifecycle: creating an instance, turning the settings into a
usable encoder, starting a bitstream, finishing one, and releasing the
instance - together with the reporting calls that describe the configuration
those steps arrived at.

A session is lame_init(), then any number of the calls under
@ref api_settings, then lame_init_params(), which decides whether the
combination is usable. Audio goes in through one of the lame_encode_buffer()
variants and the last frames come out of lame_encode_flush(); lame_close()
releases the instance.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_settings Settings
@ingroup api
@brief The encoder parameters, as set/get pairs.

Every encoder setting a caller can change is a set/get pair. The setters
record a value and validate it in isolation; whether the resulting combination
is usable is decided later, by lame_init_params().

Two properties hold throughout and are not repeated on each function:

- a setter returns 0 on success and -1 if the value was rejected or the
  instance is not usable;
- **a getter cannot report an error.** Given an unusable instance it returns
  0, which for most of these settings is also a legitimate value, so a caller
  has no way to tell the two apart. Check the instance, not the result.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_tags Tags
@ingroup api
@brief The ID3 metadata written alongside the audio.

Everything a caller can put in the ID3 tags of the encoded file: the familiar
fields, arbitrary ID3v2 frames, and cover art. These functions only record
what to write; the tags themselves are produced during encoding, or handed to
the caller on request.

Four properties hold throughout and are not repeated on each function:

- **all of this must be set first**, before lame_init_params(), since that is
  where the ID3v2 tag is assembled and it goes ahead of the audio.
- which tags are written is chosen by id3tag_init() and the handful of
  functions after it. By default LAME writes an ID3v1 tag, and an ID3v2 one as
  soon as a field that ID3v1 cannot hold is set.
- **ID3v1 is a fixed-size format with fixed-length fields.** Anything longer
  than the field is truncated there without complaint. ID3v2 has no such
  limit, so the same call can be lossy in one tag and exact in the other.
- the setters that return @c void cannot report failure at all, including a
  failure to allocate; the ones that return @c int return 0 on success and
  non-zero on failure.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_statistics Statistics
@ingroup api
@brief What the encoder did, counted while it did it.

The encoder keeps counters as it writes frames, and these report them: how the
bitrates, the stereo modes and the block types were distributed over the
encode. Reading them costs nothing and changes nothing.

Five properties hold throughout and are not repeated on each function:

- **when they can be read.** The counters live in the encoder instance, so the
  order is lame_encode_flush(), then these, then lame_close(). Anything that
  releases the instance takes the counters with it.
- **they are reset by lame_init_bitstream()**, which lame_init_params() calls
  as well - so the counts describe the current bitstream, not the process.
- **they cannot report an error.** Given an instance that is not usable they
  return having written nothing, leaving the caller's array as it was. An array
  the caller did not initialise is therefore indistinguishable from a result.
- **the 14 bitrate slots are the bitrates of the chosen MPEG version**, in the
  order the standard lists them. The 4-bit field in an MP3 frame header holds
  16 values, of which 0 means free format and 15 is invalid; the 14 that remain
  are what these arrays hold, and lame_bitrate_kbps() reports which bitrate
  each slot is. A free-format encode uses no index at all, so the entire count
  is in slot 0 and the other 13 are empty.
- **the stereo-mode slots are** 0 left/right, 1 left/right with intensity
  stereo, 2 mid/side, 3 mid/side with intensity stereo. They are counted only
  where the output has two channels; a mono encode leaves all four at zero.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_decoding Decoding
@ingroup api
@brief Reading MP3, through the optional decoder library.

libmp3lame does not decode MP3 itself. Decoding is done by libmpg123, an
external library it can be built against, and that is optional: a build
without it encodes everything it otherwise would, and decodes nothing at all.

The encoder reaches for it on its own, through none of the calls below, when
it is asked to measure its own output instead of its input - see
lame_set_decode_on_the_fly(), which has the encoder create and release a
decoder for the purpose. The functions here are for the other direction: an
application that wants to read MP3 itself, to re-encode it or to write it out
as PCM. It is the interface LAME's own frontend uses to accept MP3 input.

Two properties hold throughout and are not repeated on each function:

- the decoder is **a separate object from the encoder**. It is created by
  hip_decode_init(), passed as the first argument to everything here, and
  destroyed by hip_decode_exit(); an encoder instance is neither needed nor
  accepted.
- **a library built without libmpg123 cannot decode at all**, and says so
  where it can still be acted on: neither creation call hands back a decoder,
  so the absence is met before any input has been read.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_version Version
@ingroup api
@brief Which LAME a program is talking to.

The calls that report the version of LAME a program has linked against: a
numerical form meant for comparison, and string forms meant for display or for
embedding in an encoded stream.
*/
